===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|fatch}}, {{l|en|fotch}} {{qualifier|dialectal}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|fecchen|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{term/t|ang|feċċan}}, {{term/t|ang|fæċċan||to fetch}}. In one view, an alteration of {{term|fetian|lang=ang}}, {{term|fatian|lang=ang}} {{gloss|"to fetch, marry"; whence also English {{term|fet|lang=en}}}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*fatōną}}, {{term/t|gem-pro|*fatjaną||to fetch}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{term/t|ine-pro|*ped-||foot}}.
{{rel-top|cognates}}
 Cognate with West Frisian {{term|fetsje||to grasp|lang=fy}}, Dutch {{term|vatten||to catch, grasp, understand|lang=nl}}, German {{term|fassen||to grasp, touch|lang=de}}, {{etyl|fo|-}} {{term|fata||to grasp, understand|lang=fo}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|feta||to go, step|lang=is}}. See {{l|en|foot}}. In another view, allied to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|facian|lang=ang}}, {{term/t|ang|fācian||to acquire, get, obtain; try to obtain, wish for, desire; get to, reach}}, related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term/t|ang|fǣċan||to wish to go}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|fíkast||to desire, covet, crave|lang=is}}, and perhaps to {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{term|faka||to prepare|lang=ofs}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

