===Verb===
{{en-verb|fetches|fetching|fetched}}

# To [[retrieve]]; to bear towards; to go and [[get]].
#* Bible, 1 {{w|Books of Kings|Kings}} xvii. 11, 12
#*: He called to her, and said, '''Fetch''' me, I pray thee, a little water in a vessel, that I may drink.
#* '''1908''', {{w|Kenneth Grahame}}, ''{{w|The Wind in the Willows}}''
#*: When they got home, the Rat made a bright fire in the parlour, and planted the Mole in an arm-chair in front of it, having '''fetched''' down a dressing-gown and slippers for him, and told him river stories till supper-time.
# To obtain as price or equivalent; to sell for.
#* {{w|Thomas Babington Macaulay, 1st Baron Macaulay|Thomas Macaulay}} (1800-1859)
#*: Our native horses were held in small esteem, and '''fetched''' low prices.
#* {{quote-book|year=1913|author={{w|Joseph C. Lincoln}}|chapter=3
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=My hopes wa'n't disappointed. I never saw clams thicker than they was along them inshore flats. I filled my dreener in no time, and then it come to me that 'twouldn't be a bad idee to get a lot more, take 'em with me to Wellmouth, and peddle 'em out. Clams was fairly scarce over that side of the bay and ought to '''fetch''' a fair price.}}
#* {{quote-magazine|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent Yesterday’s fuel]
|passage=The dawn of the oil age was fairly recent. Although the stuff was used to waterproof boats in the Middle East 6,000 years ago, extracting it in earnest began only in 1859 after an oil strike in Pennsylvania. The first barrels of crude '''fetched''' $18 (around $450 at today’s prices).}}
#: {{ux|en|If you put some new tyres on it, and clean it up a bit, the car should '''fetch''' about $5,000}}
# {{label|en|nautical}} To bring or get within reach by going; to reach; to arrive at; to attain; to reach by sailing.
#* {{w|George Chapman}} (1559-1634)
#*: Meantime flew our ships, and straight we '''fetched''' / The siren's isle.
# {{label|en|intransitive}} To bring oneself; to make headway; to veer; as, to fetch about; to fetch to windward.
# {{label|en|rare|literary}} To take (a breath), to heave (a sigh)
#* '''1899''', {{w|Joseph Conrad}}, ''[[s:Heart of Darkness|Heart of Darkness]]'', [[s:Heart of Darkness/Section I|section 1]]
#*: The hurt nigger moaned feebly somewhere near by, and then '''fetched''' a deep sigh that made me mend my pace away from there.
# To cause to come; to bring to a particular state.
#* {{w|William Barnes}} (1801-1886)
#*: They couldn't '''fetch''' the butter in the churn.
# {{cx|obsolete|lang=en}} To recall from a swoon; to revive; sometimes with ''to''.
#: {{ux|en|to '''fetch''' a man to}}
#* {{w|Francis Bacon}} (1561-1626)
#*: '''Fetching''' men again when they swoon.
# To reduce; to throw.
#* {{w|Robert South}} (1634–1716)
#*: The sudden trip in wrestling that '''fetches''' a man to the ground.
# To bring to accomplishment; to achieve; to make; to perform, with certain objects.
#: {{ux|en|to '''fetch''' a compass;&emsp; to '''fetch''' a leap}}
#* {{w|William Shakespeare}} (1564-1616)
#*: I'll '''fetch''' a turn about the garden.
#* {{w|Robert South}} (1634–1716)
#*: He '''fetches''' his blow quick and sure.

